WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.100
If a mountain warms, where does a species' comfortable temperature go?

2
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.580
A species' range is the area where its populations live and reproduce.

3
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.380
Temperature falls as you climb.

4
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.680
So warming moves an isotherm, a line of equal temperature, uphill.

5
00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:28.460
If temperature sets the edge of a species' tolerable conditions, the edge should follow.

7
00:00:28.683 --> 00:00:32.623
The speed of an isotherm is called climate velocity.

8
00:00:33.403 --> 00:00:37.843
It depends on how fast the air warms, and on the lapse rate.

9
00:00:38.643 --> 00:00:42.043
The lapse rate is how fast temperature falls with height.

10
00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:47.863
Suppose a mountainside warms 0.3 degrees Celsius per decade.

11
00:00:48.803 --> 00:00:53.023
Suppose temperature falls 0.6 degrees per 100 metres.

12
00:00:54.003 --> 00:00:56.263
Both numbers are invented round figures.

13
00:00:57.063 --> 00:01:01.343
Divide the first by the second, and multiply by 100 metres.

14
00:01:02.223 --> 00:01:05.103
The isotherm climbs 50 metres per decade.

15
00:01:06.017 --> 00:01:07.837
Now add a species.

16
00:01:09.077 --> 00:01:16.137
Suppose it moved at the median from a 2011 synthesis of terrestrial species: 11 metres per decade.

18
00:01:17.137 --> 00:01:25.397
11 divided by 50 is 0.22, so the species covers 22 percent of the isotherm's shift.

19
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.520
Run it for 30 years, which is three decades.

20
00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.900
The isotherm climbs 3 times 50, or 150 metres.

21
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.760
The species climbs 3 times 11, or 33 metres.

22
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:46.640
It ends up 117 metres below the elevation that now has its former temperature.

23
00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:49.973
This holds only under assumptions.

24
00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:53.333
Warming and the lapse rate stay constant.

25
00:01:54.713 --> 00:01:56.233
Temperature alone sets the range.

26
00:01:57.173 --> 00:02:02.093
Nothing blocks the species, such as a road, a farm or a cliff.

27
00:02:03.553 --> 00:02:09.833
A real lag could reflect slow dispersal, cues other than temperature, competitors

28
00:02:09.833 --> 00:02:12.593
already upslope, or loss of forest.

29
00:02:13.753 --> 00:02:16.173
And the median hides wide variation.

30
00:02:16.983 --> 00:02:21.653
A particular species could be far ahead of the isotherm, or far behind it.

31
00:02:22.417 --> 00:02:24.697
Say a range moves uphill.

32
00:02:25.297 --> 00:02:26.957
That shows a change happened.

33
00:02:27.697 --> 00:02:29.197
Scientists call that detection.

34
00:02:29.937 --> 00:02:31.397
It does not show the cause.

35
00:02:32.617 --> 00:02:40.277
In 2,798 elevational shifts, forest loss in warmer regions tended to accelerate

36
00:02:40.277 --> 00:02:45.837
upslope movement. So an uphill shift is not proof that climate acted alone.

37
00:02:47.537 --> 00:02:51.397
Attribution links a change to a cause, and tests the alternatives.

38
00:02:51.997 --> 00:02:54.897
Test the alternatives before you name the cause.
